summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
Patient is a 30-year-old male who has been having lower back pain for about four months. He was moving boxes while his son was moving out of his college dorm and he hurt his lower back. He has had intermittent numbness and tingling in his right foot but that has resolved. He has been having pain in his lower back and has been taking tylenol and ibuprofen for the pain. He has also been having difficulty lifting heavy things and has had to change his activities. He has had no difficulty with his bladder or bowels.

Past Medical History:
He has had a total knee replacement in his right knee two years ago.

Physical Examination:
He has bony protuberance of the C5-C6 spine and no pain with palpation of the lumbar spine. He has decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise and spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine on musculoskeletal exam. His lower extremities strength is equal bilaterally.

Impression:
Acute disk herniation, most likely at the L5-S1 level. He is going to have an MRI to confirm this and to rule out any other problems. He is going to start physical therapy and is going to take meloxicam 15mg once a day for his pain. If his pain is not improving, we can discuss other treatment options such as an epidural steroid injection.

Plan:
1. MRI of the lumbar spine
2. Physical therapy
3. Meloxicam 15mg once a day
4. Follow-up appointment in one week
5. If pain is not improving, consider epidural steroid injection. }}<|eot_id|>
