summary is: {{ History of Present Illness:
Patient has had low back pain for a few weeks. It started after lifting a box of books and feels like a sharp and achy pain. Initially it was just in the lower back but now radiates down the left leg. He has had no numbness or tingling in his legs and no loss of control of his bladder or bowels. He has tried taking ibuprofen and tylenol with some relief.

Past Medical History:
Father has back pain.

Social History:
Patient is a former soccer player and has a sedentary job. He smokes and is not currently exercising. He has no other medical conditions.

Physical Examination:
Vital signs: blood pressure 130/70, pulse 80, respiratory rate 18, oxygen saturation 100%. Lung exam: clear. Heart exam: regular rate and rhythm. Abdomen: soft and non-tender. Back exam: midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Straight leg raise is positive. Neurological exam: no decreased sensation. Reflexes: patella and achilles symmetrical.

Impression:
Lumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.

Plan:
Patient will have a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and corticosteroid under fluoroscopy. This should help with the inflammation causing his pain. }}<|eot_id|>
