summary is: {{ History of Present Illness: 
The patient is a 30-year-old female who presented to the clinic with a left knee pain that started 1 week ago. She was running with her 5-year-old niece who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels and she twisted her knee in some kind of funky way. She mentioned that she heard a pop and felt a lot of pain in the medial aspect of her knee. She has been limping and has not been able to bear weight on the left leg. She has been taking tylenol and has wrapped her knee with kinesiology tape.

Past Medical History: The patient has a history of an ulcer in the past and is unable to take ibuprofen.

Physical Examination: On examination, the patient has moderate swelling and effusion in the left knee. She has moderate range of motion but is unable to fully extend and flex her knee. She has pain on flexion and extension of the knee and has a positive McMurray's sign. She also has a positive Lachman's test but a negative valgus and varus test.

Impression: The patient has a medial collateral ligament (MCL) strain in her left knee.

Plan: The doctor advised the patient to take tramadol 50mg twice a day and to take tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. She was also advised to wear a knee immobilizer for the next week and to ice her knee three to four times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time. She was advised not to run for the next three weeks and to follow up with the doctor in a month. }}<|eot_id|>
